LEAD = {
    "headline": "CROCS PUT A LIVE SHOPPING STREAM ON STAGE AT A MARKETING CONFERENCE YESTERDAY.  THREE THOUSAND PEOPLE WATCHED IT AND IT SOLD 200 ITEMS IN TWELVE MINUTES",
    "deck": "An audience of 3,000 is a number that gets a social post quietly killed in most review meetings.  Crocs has built an estimated 52.4 million dollars of TikTok Shop sales on that kind of audience, and the thing its chief brand officer says produced it is not budget.  It is the absence of an approval chain.  Which is the same trade that blew up a golf creator company two weeks ago, and worth holding both in your head at once.",
    "stamps": [
        ("ADWEEK · 16 SEP", "https://www.adweek.com/brand-marketing/how-crocs-became-one-of-tiktok-shops-top-sellers/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Terence Reilly, EVP and chief brand officer at Crocs, spoke at Adweek's Brandweek in Atlanta and then did the unusual thing of demonstrating the strategy live rather than describing it.  He invited presenter Liana Gibbs on stage to host a TikTok Shop livestream in front of the room.  <mark>About 3,000 people tuned in.  During the twelve-minute demo, Crocs sold 200 items.</mark>  Products shown included the vegan Chalet Clog and the Ballet Flat.",
        "Hold that ratio for a second, because it is the whole item.  Three thousand viewers is a rounding error on any reach plan you have ever seen.  It is the sort of number that gets a channel described as not worth the production cost.  And it moved 200 units in the length of a coffee break, in a conference hall, with a presenter who does not do this for a living.",
        "The context is the scale it has already produced.  <mark>Crocs racked up an estimated 52.4 million dollars in TikTok Shop sales in the twelve months to April</mark>, making it the top footwear seller on the platform, according to data intelligence firm Charm.io.  TikTok Shop named it Seller of the Year in May.  The brand has over 3 million TikTok followers.  It signed on as an early adopter of TikTok Shop in 2024.",
        "The single most useful number Reilly gave is the one nobody will put on a slide.  <mark>Croctober was a 31-day, 744-hour livestream marathon.</mark>  That is 24 hours a day for a month.  It tripled Crocs' follower count, generated 550 million impressions, and won a Shorty Award.  Do the arithmetic on what 744 hours of airtime would cost you as media and you get a number no brand would sign off.  Do it as owned production and it is a rota.",
        "Reilly's own explanation of how any of it got made is organisational and slightly rude.  When we got started on TikTok, we didn't know what we were doing, he said.  We'd like to pretend we're really smart.  We have a whole bunch of experts.  No, we had a whole bunch of people who cared and were willing to try.  Willing to fail, and then willing to scale.  His advice to other brands: do not build bureaucracy.  Give them permission to fail, and then give them permission to scale.  If there are too many meetings, you're losing.  If there are too many people, you're losing.",
        "Now read that against the L'S section below, because the same doctrine is what produced this week's worst brand story.  Good Good shipped a 56-second ad that cost its chief executive, its president, its shelf space at Dick's and a PGA Tour title sponsorship, and one person who has worked with the company told Business Insider they had seen ads fly out the door with little oversight.  Speed is not a strategy on its own.  It is a bet that the cost of the bad ones stays smaller than the value of the volume, and the two brands are the two outcomes of that same bet.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("200", "items sold in a twelve-minute demo livestream watched by about 3,000 people"),
        ("52.4m", "dollars of estimated TikTok Shop sales in the twelve months to April, per Charm.io"),
        ("744", "hours of continuous live video in one month, which tripled the follower count"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Everything here except the sales estimate came from a Crocs executive on stage at Adweek's own event, with no independent verification.  The 52.4 million dollar figure is an estimate from Charm.io, not a company-reported or audited number, and Adweek labels it as such.  The 3,000-viewer, 200-item demo was staged for a conference audience who were watching the stage at the same time, so it is not a clean read on what a normal Crocs livestream converts at.  The 550 million impressions and tripled follower count for Croctober are Crocs' own figures with no base disclosed.",
    "so_what": "The received idea is that live shopping needs scale to work, so you wait until you can promote it properly and then you never run one.  What the demo actually shows is that live is a conversion format, not a reach format, and the two are priced completely differently in your head.  Three thousand people who chose to watch a shopping stream are not the same three thousand people as a post impression, and treating both as audience is how brands end up cancelling the thing that sells and funding the thing that gets seen.",
    "do_this": "Book one live shopping stream in the next three weeks with a host who actually knows the products, and judge it on items sold per minute rather than on viewers.  Write the target down before you start, as units and minutes, so nobody in the review can move the goalposts to reach afterwards.",
}

